# Link cards

Turns a bare external link that sits on its own line into a "link card" - a
box showing the target page's title, description, favicon and (when it has
one) a thumbnail image, in the style of the preview cards you see when you
paste a URL into a chat app.

## What gets transformed

A link is only converted when all of these hold:

- the visible link text is exactly the URL (a bare link, not `[some words](url)`)
- the URL is `http:` or `https:` with no embedded credentials
- the host is not the blog's own domain
- if the link is inside a paragraph, that paragraph contains nothing else

So in a Markdown post:

```
# Title of post

https://www.nngroup.com/articles/cards-component/

Some text here
```

the middle line renders as a card while everything else is untouched. A link
in the middle of a sentence, a link with custom text, and an internal link
are all left alone.

At most 25 links per entry are turned into cards, fetched a few at a time, so
a page full of dead links can't stall the build.

## Options

- `layout` - `compact` (default: thumbnail beside the text) or `large`
  (thumbnail stacked above the text). The dashboard form writes the matching
  `layoutCompact` / `layoutLarge` booleans for the template to read.

The plugin is on by default for new blogs.

## Fetching and caching

Metadata is scraped from the target page's Open Graph / Twitter Card / `<meta>`
tags. The favicon and the OG image are downloaded into the blog's static
folder (`_link_cards/` for the metadata JSON, `link_cards/` for images and
`link_cards/icons/` for favicons) and the image is resized into a
`srcset` (240 / 480 / 960 px) with `sharp`.

Results are cached per URL by content hash via `helper/transformer`. A lookup
that fails (site down, non-2xx, blocked) is cached as a negative result for
six hours and served as a hostname-only card, so a broken link isn't
re-fetched on every rebuild.

## Security

The target URL and every asset URL derived from the fetched page are
user-controlled, so all outbound requests go through the airlock proxy
(`helper/airlock`) - the same egress boundary used by the screenshot and
transformer download paths. In production a missing airlock config makes the
fetch fail (and the card falls back to hostname-only) rather than connecting
directly. URLs carrying credentials and non-`http(s)` asset URLs are rejected
before any request is made.

## Google Docs

`docs.google.com` / `drive.google.com` links don't expose Open Graph tags,
and a doc that isn't world-readable redirects to a localised sign-in page.
Rather than scrape that, these links are recognised by URL and rendered as a
plain branded card ("Google Docs" / "Google Sheets" / ...). A public doc's
name is used as the title when the page still reports it
(`<Doc name> - Google Docs`, or a published `/pub` link).
